<title>USA: U.S. April construction spending fell 1.0 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. April construction spending fell 1.0 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-06-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Commerce Department seasonally adjusted construction spending, with comparisons.</p>
<p> PERCENT CHANGE:   April     Mar--(Prev)   Feb-(Prev) Apr97/96</p>
<p> Total Spending     -1.0     0.1   -0.2    2.3    2.1	5.7</p>
<p> ANNUAL RATES, BILLIONS OF U.S. DOLLARS:		    Apr'96</p>
<p> Total Spending    596.9   603.1  600.1  602.7  601.4    564.6</p>
<p> Private Spending  450.2   453.7  451.9  455.6  454.5    424.2</p>
<p> -Residential	258.2   257.8  256.6  255.4  254.1    248.0</p>
<p> --New Housing     182.1   182.5  182.4  182.3  182.0    179.3</p>
<p> ---Single-Family  160.1   160.0  159.2  159.1  158.6    156.8</p>
<p> ---Multi-Family    21.9    22.5   23.2   23.3   23.3     22.5</p>
<p>ANNUAL RATES, BILLIONS OF U.S. DOLLARS:</p>
<p> -		     April     Mar--(Prev)   Feb-(Prev)   Apr'96</p>
<p> -Nonresidential   150.9   154.9  153.8  157.2  157.4    135.5</p>
<p> --Industrial	 29.0    28.4   28.1   30.2   30.1     30.3</p>
<p> --Office	     27.9    28.9   29.4   30.8   30.9     22.1</p>
<p> Public Spending   146.7   149.4  148.2  147.1  146.9    140.4</p>
<p> PERCENT CHANGE:   April     Mar--(Prev)   Feb-(Prev) Apr97/96</p>
<p> Private Spending   -0.8    -0.4   -0.6    2.0    1.8	6.1</p>
<p> Public Spending    -1.8     1.6    0.9    3.4    3.3	4.5</p>
<p>Construction spending in 1992 dollars, which discounts the effects of inflation:</p>
<p> PERCENT CHANGE:   April     Mar--(Prev)   Feb--(Prev)</p>
<p>  Total Spending    -1.3    -0.3   -0.6    2.3    2.3</p>
<p>ANNUAL RATES, BILLIONS OF U.S. DOLLARS:</p>
<p>  Total Spending   511.3   517.8  516.2  519.4  519.4</p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast overall spending on construction to rise 0.3 percent in April.  </p>
<p>The drop in April construction spending was the first monthly decline since a 1 percent decline in December. The last month in which construction spending dropped more than it did in April was nearly a year ago, in May 1996, when it fell 1.1 percent.</p>
<p>Privately financed residential building projects hit a record $258.2 rate in April, up 0.2 percent from March. Spending on single-family home building rose slightly to a record $160.1 billion annual rate, from $160 billion in March.</p>
